<DocsLayout title="Evidence & gates" description="Turn data, model, reliability, and release claims into reviewable evidence." current="evidence">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Proof before promotion</p>
  <h1>Make the claim.<br /><em>Show the evidence.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">A green training run is not a release decision. scmle records the raw inputs, reproducible command, artifact, reviewer, limitations, and disposition behind each claim.</p>

  <div class="callout"><p><strong>The rule:</strong> implemented means code exists; supported means the declared lifecycle passes; production-proven means an independent reviewer reproduced the evidence from a clean checkout.</p></div>

  <h2>One claim, one evidence record</h2>
  <p>Every release criterion is a named record. Do not write “quality checked” when the useful claim is “the curation report contains a structured finding for every detected issue.” The record should make the claim falsifiable.</p>
  <pre><code>{`criterion_id:
  claim: structured curation findings are persisted
  scope: {release: beta, area: data}
  raw_inputs: {dataset: sha256:...}
  command_or_test: uv run pytest tests/integration/test_quality_checks_in_run.py
  artifact: {path: curation_report.json, digest: sha256:...}
  result: pass
  reviewer: independent-reviewer
  observed_at: 2026-10-20T12:00:00+00:00
  limitations: [range-backed and warehouse snapshot identity remains transport-owned]
  decision: accepted`}</code></pre>

  <h2>How the gates work</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Declare the boundary:</strong> a capability names its modality, task, model family, evaluation, uncertainty, fairness, explainability, and inference surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Collect raw evidence:</strong> keep dataset snapshots, test output, artifact digests, resource measurements, and failure traces.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluate the claim:</strong> compare results with explicit thresholds, uncertainty, subgroup warnings, and known limitations.</li>
    <li><strong>Review independently:</strong> reproduce from a clean checkout and recompute at least one critical result.</li>
    <li><strong>Promote or demote:</strong> accept the capability, block the release, or mark it extension-only. Never hide an incomplete stage.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Read fairness evidence without overclaiming</h2>
  <p>The fairness report evaluates each declared sensitive attribute and, when two or more are declared, the intersection of those attributes. It reports selection, error, and bias differences for cells that meet the minimum sample threshold. Cells below that threshold are not silently treated as evidence: their observed counts appear in <code>excluded_groups</code> and <code>power_warning</code> is set to <code>true</code>.</p>
  <pre><code>{`fairness.json
├── columns.group
├── columns.region
└── columns.intersectional
    ├── groups: "group=a | region=north"
    ├── excluded_groups: { ... }
    └── power_warning: true | false`}</code></pre>
  <p>A disparity flag is a decision-support signal, not a causal conclusion. Check the cell counts, label prevalence, uncertainty, measurement process, and domain policy before deciding whether to mitigate or block promotion.</p>

  <h2>Read diagnostics as evidence, not decoration</h2>
  <p>The evaluation evidence includes two bounded diagnostics that answer different questions. <code>error_analysis</code> identifies the held-out rows the model handled least well. <code>pairwise_interaction</code> asks whether the model output changes differently when two numeric features move together than when each feature moves alone. Both are evidence attached to the exact evaluation input and model; neither is a causal explanation.</p>
  <div class="split-cards">
    <div class="mini-card">
      <span class="mini-label">01 / Error analysis</span>
      <h3>Where did this model fail?</h3>
      <p>Classification returns bounded lists of row IDs for misclassified or low-confidence predictions, with actual and predicted labels, confidence, and the misclassification count. Regression returns the largest absolute residuals, actual and predicted values, residuals, and the evaluation MAE.</p>
      <p class="note">Raw feature values are intentionally excluded. Join row IDs to an access-controlled source only when the privacy policy permits it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mini-card">
      <span class="mini-label">02 / Pairwise interaction</span>
      <h3>Does the model combine signals?</h3>
      <p>The diagnostic computes a bounded second-order output difference around evaluation medians for numeric feature pairs. A non-zero value means the fitted model behaves non-additively in that local representation space; it does not establish mechanism, causation, or domain interaction.</p>
      <p class="note">The default budget is at most 10 numeric features and 200 evaluation rows. Deadline or budget exhaustion is recorded as truncation rather than silently presented as complete coverage.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <pre><code>{`run.diagnostics
├── error_analysis
│   ├── classification: {row_id, actual, predicted, confidence}
│   └── regression: {row_id, actual, predicted, residual, absolute_error}
└── pairwise_interaction
    ├── feature_pair: [feature_a, feature_b]
    ├── interaction_score
    └── limitations: [feature_space, associational, numeric_only, bounded]`}</code></pre>
  <p>Reviewers should inspect the row count, truncation state, evaluation split, model digest, and limitations before interpreting either diagnostic. An error list is not a root-cause analysis; an interaction score is not a fairness or causal finding. A release policy may require these records to exist, but their presence alone cannot turn a weak model into an approved one.</p>

  <h2>What blocks a release</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A supported modality is missing evidence for any of its 15 lifecycle stages.</li>
    <li>A required metric, diagnostic, fairness result, uncertainty result, or explanation is absent.</li>
    <li>A fault scenario has no verified safe outcome or recovery evidence.</li>
    <li>A dataset snapshot, model bundle, or dependency cannot be identified and reproduced.</li>
    <li>A reviewer cannot distinguish a warning from a release-blocking finding.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Generated controls are not approvals</h2>
  <p>Run artifacts make review possible, but they do not replace review. A dataset card starts as <code>machine_generated_unreviewed</code>; the fault-injection plan starts as <code>planned</code> and release-blocking; and a missing evaluation policy produces <code>policy_not_declared</code>. Those states are safer than inventing a threshold or implying that a checklist was executed.</p>

  <h2>Independent review is a separate proof</h2>
  <p><code>release_evidence.json</code> is itself versioned and HMAC-signed when <code>SCMLE_SIGNING_KEY</code> is configured. The release evaluator verifies that signature before accepting the evidence index, and the run lineage records its digest. This protects the evidence collection from being edited after the run; it does not replace signer custody, key rotation, or independent review.</p>
  <p><code>IndependentReleaseReview</code> is the signed record for the final human or external review. It binds the reviewer to the exact source commit, clean-clone and environment digests, reproducible commands, artifact digests, and independently recomputed metric, fairness, and explanation claims. The reviewer must not appear in the implementer set, every claim must pass, and the decision must be approved. A valid record proves the review contract; it does not manufacture an external reviewer or turn a local rehearsal into production evidence.</p>
  <pre><code>{`review.accepted(
    source_commit="&lt;exact-release-sha&gt;",
    required_kinds=("metric", "fairness", "explanation"),
    signing_key="&lt;review-key&gt;",
)`}</code></pre>
  <p>For a reviewer who has checked out the exact source, the persisted record can be verified without opening the application:</p>
  <pre><code>{`SCMLE_REVIEW_SIGNING_KEY="..." \\
  python scripts/verify_release_review.py release/independent-review.json \\
  --source-commit "&lt;exact-release-sha&gt;"`}</code></pre>
  <p>A zero exit status proves that the signed record is accepted for that commit and required claim set. It still does not create the reviewer identity, recompute the claims, or prove that the external environment was independent; those are inputs to the record.</p>

  <h2>Read the evidence in order</h2>
  <div class="diagram">
    <div class="diagram-caption"><span>Evidence path</span><strong>raw source to release decision</strong></div>
    <div class="data-path" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Evidence path diagram, scrolls sideways on narrow screens">
